<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Extra WiX source for the `client` component MSI (TASK-148), linked in via
  -D CPACK_WIX_EXTRA_SOURCES alongside CPack's auto-generated fragments.

  Adds:
    1. The register-client-task.ps1 helper as an installed file (deployed
       next to vapourwault-daemon.exe, under the same auto-generated
       CM_DP_client.bin directory CPack created for the client component's
       binaries).
    2. A pair of deferred CustomActions (register / unregister) that invoke
       it directly by relative filename, with Directory="CM_DP_client.bin"
       as their working directory.
    3. InstallExecuteSequence entries scheduling registration after files
       are installed (skipped on REMOVE, i.e. not run again on repair/
       uninstall) and unregistration before files are removed (only when
       REMOVE~="ALL" - a genuine uninstall, not an in-place upgrade,
       matching the MSI convention used throughout Windows Installer
       authoring for "only run cleanup on final removal").

  NOT run with Return="ignore" by accident: a scheduled-task registration
  failure should not abort the whole (otherwise successful) file install —
  the manual PowerShell script remains available as a fallback per
  TASK-145's design. This is a deliberate best-effort tradeoff, not an
  oversight; flagged for SEC.07/QA review (TASK-153/TASK-152) since a
  silent failure here means "files installed but daemon never
  auto-starts," which could go unnoticed. `Impersonate="yes"` matches the
  MSI convention for actions that should run as the acting interactive
  user, which for this PER-USER (no elevation) install context is the
  session's own user throughout anyway (see TASK-145's SEC.07-resolved
  elevation-scope requirement).

  NO [CustomActionData] TOKEN HERE, DELIBERATELY (TASK-247): the original
  authoring passed -DaemonExe/-StateDir into register-client-task.ps1 via
  the standard immediate-action-sets-a-property / deferred-action-reads-
  [CustomActionData] two-step pattern. Found for real (TASK-152/247) that
  this project's exact WiX v3.11.2 + msiexec combination never actually
  delivers CustomActionData to this deferred action at all: the token
  resolves to an empty string at real execution time. Confirmed by
  redirecting the literal invoked command's own stdout to a file (not
  just reading the MSI log's property-table dump, which always looks
  correct since it only shows what the immediate action set, never what
  the deferred action actually received — a red herring on its own).
  Ruled out, each verified independently and still reproducing empty:
  Impersonate on/off, an all-uppercase (public) property name (MSI only
  carries a *public*, i.e. entirely-uppercase, property through to the
  deferred script — mixed case is silently dropped, but fixing the
  casing alone did not fix this), a single-word value with no spaces or
  quotes (rules out a quoting/escaping issue), and both a direct elevated
  `msiexec /i` and a de-elevated one via a temporary scheduled task
  (rules out the test harness itself). Rather than keep chasing an
  unexplained engine-level quirk, register-client-task.ps1 no longer
  needs any templated argument at all: it derives its own daemon path
  from $PSScriptRoot and its own state directory from %APPDATA%, both of
  which are fixed, well-known values that never vary per install. The
  CustomActions below invoke it by plain relative filename with
  Directory="CM_DP_client.bin" (the actual bin directory, a real WiX
  Directory Id) as the working directory — sidestepping the
  CustomActionData mechanism entirely instead of depending on it.

  STILL FAILS, FOR A DIFFERENT AND DEEPER REASON (TASK-250): once
  CustomActionData was out of the picture, REGISTERCLIENTTASK's real
  failure became visible for the first time (verified via a temporary
  cmd.exe-wrapped stdout/stderr redirect, since Return="ignore" swallows
  it otherwise): `Register-ScheduledTask : Access is denied` at
  register-client-task.ps1's own Register-ScheduledTask call — even
  though the exact same script, run manually and interactively as the
  same user immediately afterward, succeeds and registers the task with
  the correct UserId/RunLevel/LogonType. This is a genuine, documented
  Windows Task Scheduler limitation, not something Impersonate="yes" can
  fix: the COM API backing Register-ScheduledTask requires the caller to
  be running inside the user's actual interactive logon session, and an
  MSI deferred custom action's impersonated token — however correctly
  impersonated — is still hosted by the msiexec service process, which
  has no such session attached. This is a different, deeper problem than
  the earlier session's Group-Policy-blocked-ONLOGON-tasks finding
  (TASK-148's note): that was one specific machine's policy; this
  reproduces on an unrestricted personal session too. Left as Return=
  "ignore" (unchanged from the original design) since forcing this to be
  fatal wouldn't make it succeed; TASK-250 covers the real architectural
  fix (most likely Windows' "Active Setup" mechanism — HKLM Active Setup
  Installed Components with a StubPath — which Windows itself re-runs
  once per user at their next real interactive logon, the one context
  where this actually works).

  ONE Fragment, deliberately (not split across two, as an earlier revision
  had it): light.exe drops any Fragment nothing else references (its
  dead-Fragment elimination is per-Fragment, not per-entity), which a real
  install test caught (TASK-152) after the split version compiled and
  linked with zero errors/warnings but silently produced an MSI whose
  CustomAction table had NEITHER of these two actions in it at all, since
  nothing referenced that second fragment specifically. Referencing the
  Component below (client-patch.xml's ComponentRef) is what pulls this
  whole Fragment in, CustomActions and InstallExecuteSequence included;
  splitting them back out into their own Fragment would silently
  reintroduce the exact same bug.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">

  <Fragment>
    <DirectoryRef Id="CM_DP_client.bin">
      <!-- Fixed GUID (not "*"): WiX cannot auto-generate a component guid
           for a component that mixes a registry-value KeyPath with a
           File resource (CNDL0230). This fixed value must never change
           once shipped. -->
      <Component Id="CM_C_ClientTaskScript" Guid="F5D76859-3F79-4193-BA72-ED6107ED115A">
        <!-- Per-user-directory components must use an HKCU registry value
             as their KeyPath, not a file (WiX ICE38), matching this
             package's per-user (CPACK_WIX_ROOT_FOLDER_ID=LocalAppDataFolder)
             install scope. -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
            Key="Software\VaporWault\vaporwault\Components"
            Name="CM_C_ClientTaskScript"
            Type="string"
            Value="1"
            KeyPath="yes" />
        <File Id="CM_F_ClientTaskScript"
              Source="$(var.VW_WIX_DIR)\register-client-task.ps1" />
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!-- Register (install / reinstall / repair, never on uninstall). No
         arguments: the script derives its own daemon path and state
         directory (see register-client-task.ps1's own header comment). -->
    <CustomAction Id="REGISTERCLIENTTASK"
        Directory="CM_DP_client.bin"
        Execute="deferred"
        Return="ignore"
        Impersonate="yes"
        ExeCommand="powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File register-client-task.ps1" />

    <!-- Unregister (uninstall only, not upgrade/repair). -->
    <CustomAction Id="UNREGISTERCLIENTTASK"
        Directory="CM_DP_client.bin"
        Execute="deferred"
        Return="ignore"
        Impersonate="yes"
        ExeCommand="powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File register-client-task.ps1 -Uninstall" />

    <!-- Forced cleanup on full uninstall (TASK-247): this per-user install's
         three CPack-generated binary Components (vapourwault-cli.exe,
         vapourwault-daemon.exe, vapourwault-gui.exe) still use a plain
         File KeyPath rather than the HKCU-registry KeyPath WiX ICE38 wants
         for a user-profile install. CPACK_WIX_PATCH_FILE can only ADD
         child elements to CPack's auto-generated Components, never modify
         or remove their existing KeyPath (verified empirically: patching
         in a RegistryValue KeyPath alongside the existing File KeyPath
         hits CNDL0042 "multiple key paths", and there is no way through
         the patch mechanism to flip the File's own KeyPath off first). At
         real install/uninstall test (TASK-152), MSI's native per-user
         component-removal tracking did not clean these files up on
         `msiexec /x` (ICE38/64/91 are suppressed for this build, see
         docs/RELEASE.md, and this is exactly the functional gap those
         ICEs exist to flag, not just a lint nicety). Deleting the whole
         install directory explicitly here is deterministic and doesn't
         depend on MSI's per-component tracking working correctly for
         this specific per-user edge case. Scoped to REMOVE~="ALL" only
         (never on upgrade/repair) and ordered After="RemoveFiles" so it
         runs once MSI's own (best-effort) file removal has already had
         its turn. -->
    <!-- Directory="INSTALL_ROOT" (not TARGETDIR) puts this action's own
         working directory at the real per-user install root, resolved by
         msiexec itself before launch, unlike a [Property]/
         [CustomActionData] token inside ExeCommand (proven broken above),
         a CustomAction's Directory attribute genuinely does resolve for a
         deferred action (confirmed: REGISTERCLIENTTASK's own
         Directory="CM_DP_client.bin" correctly sets its working directory
         to the real bin folder). So this command asks PowerShell for ITS
         OWN current directory at runtime (Get-Location), which is exactly
         that resolved path, rather than needing any WiX token at all. -->
    <CustomAction Id="REMOVECLIENTINSTALLDIR"
        Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
        Execute="deferred"
        Return="ignore"
        Impersonate="yes"
        ExeCommand="powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command &quot;$p = (Get-Location).Path; Set-Location $env:TEMP; Remove-Item -LiteralPath $p -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue&quot;" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="REGISTERCLIENTTASK" After="InstallFiles">NOT REMOVE</Custom>
      <Custom Action="UNREGISTERCLIENTTASK" Before="RemoveFiles">REMOVE~="ALL"</Custom>
      <Custom Action="REMOVECLIENTINSTALLDIR" After="RemoveFiles">REMOVE~="ALL"</Custom>
    </InstallExecuteSequence>
  </Fragment>

</Wix>
